1
00:00:00,140 --> 00:00:03,630
Welcome back. In
an earlier video,

2
00:00:03,630 --> 00:00:04,920
we learned about how to

3
00:00:04,920 --> 00:00:07,395
identify risks when
planning projects.

4
00:00:07,395 --> 00:00:10,920
In this module, we'll learn
why risks and changes might

5
00:00:10,920 --> 00:00:12,570
occur during a project and how

6
00:00:12,570 --> 00:00:14,895
that can impact your
project's scope.

7
00:00:14,895 --> 00:00:17,310
You may remember that a risk is

8
00:00:17,310 --> 00:00:19,260
a potential event that might

9
00:00:19,260 --> 00:00:21,530
occur and could
impact your project.

10
00:00:21,530 --> 00:00:22,920
When you think about risks in

11
00:00:22,920 --> 00:00:24,900
the context of
project management,

12
00:00:24,900 --> 00:00:27,360
you'll think about
them as hypothetical.

13
00:00:27,360 --> 00:00:29,280
In other words, these might not

14
00:00:29,280 --> 00:00:31,200
be events that will
definitely happen,

15
00:00:31,200 --> 00:00:33,990
but because there's a possibility
that they could happen,

16
00:00:33,990 --> 00:00:36,735
it's your responsibility
as a project manager

17
00:00:36,735 --> 00:00:39,120
to identify and plan
for those risks.

18
00:00:39,120 --> 00:00:42,110
Let's revisit some
examples of risks.

19
00:00:42,111 --> 00:00:42,965
A project risk might be

20
00:00:42,965 --> 00:00:44,630
a contractor missing a deadline,

21
00:00:44,630 --> 00:00:46,880
or introducing a tool that may

22
00:00:46,880 --> 00:00:49,250
lead to communication
breakdown within your team,

23
00:00:49,250 --> 00:00:51,500
or unexpected,
additional work because

24
00:00:51,500 --> 00:00:54,130
of an unforeseen policy
being put in place.

25
00:00:54,130 --> 00:00:55,925
When any risk occurs,

26
00:00:55,925 --> 00:00:59,045
the consequence is a change
to the project plan.

27
00:00:59,045 --> 00:01:03,019
A change is anything that
alters or impacts the tasks,

28
00:01:03,019 --> 00:01:05,840
structures, or processes
within a project.

29
00:01:05,840 --> 00:01:08,330
Changes are typically unexpected.

30
00:01:08,330 --> 00:01:09,845
More often than not,

31
00:01:09,845 --> 00:01:12,230
they have a negative
impact on the project,

32
00:01:12,230 --> 00:01:14,285
and you'll have to
learn to navigate that.

33
00:01:14,285 --> 00:01:16,250
But sometimes, and I'm placing

34
00:01:16,250 --> 00:01:18,995
a heavy emphasis on sometimes,

35
00:01:18,995 --> 00:01:21,355
changes can have a
positive impact.

36
00:01:21,355 --> 00:01:23,720
Changes can encompass
any variance from

37
00:01:23,720 --> 00:01:25,130
the original project plan in

38
00:01:25,130 --> 00:01:27,170
regards to the triple constraint.

39
00:01:27,170 --> 00:01:30,125
This may entail changing
priorities and scope,

40
00:01:30,125 --> 00:01:33,625
budget and resources, or changes
to the project timeline.

41
00:01:33,625 --> 00:01:36,890
We'll discuss how the internal
and external dependencies

42
00:01:36,890 --> 00:01:40,405
of a project impact each
other and bring about change.

43
00:01:40,405 --> 00:01:42,380
Let's go through a few types of

44
00:01:42,380 --> 00:01:44,315
changes that can
affect your project.

45
00:01:44,315 --> 00:01:46,190
Some examples of changes may

46
00:01:46,190 --> 00:01:48,680
include new or
changing dependencies.

47
00:01:48,680 --> 00:01:51,080
Dependencies are
tasks, activities,

48
00:01:51,080 --> 00:01:54,205
or milestones that are
reliant on one another.

49
00:01:54,205 --> 00:01:56,735
So if one task isn't
completed on time,

50
00:01:56,735 --> 00:01:59,105
it may put your
other tasks behind.

51
00:01:59,105 --> 00:02:00,800
You might be in
charge of renovations

52
00:02:00,800 --> 00:02:02,920
on a home where there
are dependencies.

53
00:02:02,920 --> 00:02:05,180
In a bathroom remodel,
for instance,

54
00:02:05,180 --> 00:02:07,085
a new sink cannot be installed

55
00:02:07,085 --> 00:02:09,310
until the vanity and
plumbing are in place.

56
00:02:09,310 --> 00:02:11,465
Next, is changing priorities.

57
00:02:11,465 --> 00:02:13,640
The scope of the
remodel changes if

58
00:02:13,640 --> 00:02:16,070
your client's in-laws
suddenly have to move in,

59
00:02:16,070 --> 00:02:17,930
and you have to move
up planned work on

60
00:02:17,930 --> 00:02:21,205
the spare bedroom before
completing the bathroom remodel.

61
00:02:21,205 --> 00:02:24,695
Next up, the capacity and
people available could change.

62
00:02:24,695 --> 00:02:26,765
Maybe you have to
replace the plumber

63
00:02:26,765 --> 00:02:29,100
because you had issues
on the job site.

64
00:02:29,100 --> 00:02:31,235
Another type of
change could include

65
00:02:31,235 --> 00:02:33,890
a new limitation on your
budget or resources.

66
00:02:33,890 --> 00:02:35,810
For example, you need to

67
00:02:35,810 --> 00:02:38,300
reduce design costs
in the new bathroom

68
00:02:38,300 --> 00:02:40,490
by 10 percent because
your quotes for

69
00:02:40,490 --> 00:02:43,010
electrical work come in
higher than expected.

70
00:02:43,010 --> 00:02:45,255
Another change could
be scope creep.

71
00:02:45,255 --> 00:02:47,375
Scope creep is when changes,

72
00:02:47,375 --> 00:02:50,500
growth, and other factors
affect the project scope.

73
00:02:50,500 --> 00:02:53,270
For instance, your clients
are so happy with the tile in

74
00:02:53,270 --> 00:02:54,680
the new bathroom
that they'd like to

75
00:02:54,680 --> 00:02:57,185
replace the tile in all
of their bathrooms.

76
00:02:57,185 --> 00:02:59,135
Finally, force majeure.

77
00:02:59,135 --> 00:03:01,400
This is another change
that could occur due

78
00:03:01,400 --> 00:03:03,805
to a national or
international crisis.

79
00:03:03,805 --> 00:03:05,840
If you aren't familiar
with this term,

80
00:03:05,840 --> 00:03:08,810
it means an unforeseen
circumstance that prevents

81
00:03:08,810 --> 00:03:10,595
someone from fulfilling
the contract

82
00:03:10,595 --> 00:03:12,415
due to a major crisis.

83
00:03:12,415 --> 00:03:14,715
Force majeure is pretty uncommon.

84
00:03:14,715 --> 00:03:17,780
But for instance, if a
union goes on strike,

85
00:03:17,780 --> 00:03:20,870
certain vendors won't be able
to fulfill their contracts.

86
00:03:20,870 --> 00:03:22,355
If there's a pandemic,

87
00:03:22,355 --> 00:03:25,555
all production on your new
product might be halted.

88
00:03:25,555 --> 00:03:27,860
Changes should be
measured against

89
00:03:27,860 --> 00:03:29,915
the baseline estimates of scope,

90
00:03:29,915 --> 00:03:31,580
budget, and time allotted for

91
00:03:31,580 --> 00:03:34,250
your project, given the
original requirements.

92
00:03:34,250 --> 00:03:37,650
Be mindful that when you change
any one of those things,

93
00:03:37,650 --> 00:03:39,560
there may be knock-on
effects which

94
00:03:39,560 --> 00:03:41,740
could be positive or negative.

95
00:03:41,740 --> 00:03:44,270
For instance, your
clients may believe they

96
00:03:44,270 --> 00:03:46,685
have beautiful hardwood
floors hiding underneath

97
00:03:46,685 --> 00:03:49,280
old living room carpet
and want to pull up

98
00:03:49,280 --> 00:03:50,540
the old carpet and use

99
00:03:50,540 --> 00:03:52,465
the original hardwood
floors underneath.

100
00:03:52,465 --> 00:03:54,020
You, the project manager

101
00:03:54,020 --> 00:03:55,825
on this particular
construction job—you've

102
00:03:55,826 --> 00:03:58,490
budgeted to have
the carpet removed and

103
00:03:58,490 --> 00:04:01,740
the old floors sanded
and stained. Bad news.

104
00:04:01,740 --> 00:04:03,410
You pull up the carpet and find

105
00:04:03,410 --> 00:04:05,615
the floors are in bad
shape and rotting.

106
00:04:05,615 --> 00:04:07,415
They'll need to be
replaced or repaired,

107
00:04:07,415 --> 00:04:08,900
which could be costly,

108
00:04:08,900 --> 00:04:11,650
so your timeline and budget
are likely to take a hit.

109
00:04:11,650 --> 00:04:13,370
When it comes to who takes on

110
00:04:13,370 --> 00:04:15,770
the responsibility of
managing the changing scope,

111
00:04:15,770 --> 00:04:17,900
it'll be you—the
project manager.

112
00:04:17,900 --> 00:04:19,415
But depending on the project,

113
00:04:19,415 --> 00:04:21,580
you probably won't do it alone.

114
00:04:21,580 --> 00:04:23,655
In order to properly
manage changes,

115
00:04:23,655 --> 00:04:25,460
you'll want to refer
to documents like

116
00:04:25,460 --> 00:04:27,610
your Statement of Work
and the RACI chart.

117
00:04:27,610 --> 00:04:30,980
But you might also have to
create some new documentation.

118
00:04:30,980 --> 00:04:33,740
You'll want to create or
familiarize yourself with

119
00:04:33,740 --> 00:04:35,300
the processes for requesting

120
00:04:35,300 --> 00:04:38,150
changes for your team
or organization.

121
00:04:38,150 --> 00:04:42,095
These processes might include
a change request form.

122
00:04:42,095 --> 00:04:44,945
Let's discuss change
request forms.

123
00:04:44,945 --> 00:04:46,475
You and your stakeholders will

124
00:04:46,475 --> 00:04:48,170
use these forms in order to stay

125
00:04:48,170 --> 00:04:51,845
on top of, and adequately
manage, any changes.

126
00:04:51,845 --> 00:04:54,200
Since a lot of people
with different roles

127
00:04:54,200 --> 00:04:56,060
on the project can
fill out these forms,

128
00:04:56,060 --> 00:04:57,800
it's important for
the forms to be

129
00:04:57,800 --> 00:05:00,340
self-explanatory
and very thorough.

130
00:05:00,340 --> 00:05:01,895
In the provided template,

131
00:05:01,895 --> 00:05:04,145
which uses a 2-by-10 table,

132
00:05:04,145 --> 00:05:06,530
you'll need to include
information in the cells,

133
00:05:06,530 --> 00:05:09,140
such as: the project name,

134
00:05:09,140 --> 00:05:11,180
the discussion owner, who's

135
00:05:11,180 --> 00:05:13,370
taking the lead on this
discussion from the team,

136
00:05:13,370 --> 00:05:15,440
discussion type.
You'll want to let

137
00:05:15,440 --> 00:05:17,750
your audience know if you'll
be discussing a risk,

138
00:05:17,750 --> 00:05:20,150
opportunity, or anything else.

139
00:05:20,150 --> 00:05:22,070
You'll want to identify the teams

140
00:05:22,070 --> 00:05:24,920
involved and the expected
outcome of the discussion,

141
00:05:24,920 --> 00:05:28,100
which might be a change in
priorities, schedule change,

142
00:05:28,100 --> 00:05:30,110
or an official call
on how to proceed

143
00:05:30,110 --> 00:05:32,240
with an issue. Add
the target date for

144
00:05:32,240 --> 00:05:34,595
discussion, and identify
which milestones

145
00:05:34,595 --> 00:05:36,625
or goals might be impacted.

146
00:05:36,625 --> 00:05:38,510
Provide a short description of

147
00:05:38,510 --> 00:05:40,580
the current situation,
the change,

148
00:05:40,580 --> 00:05:41,690
and any difference you

149
00:05:41,690 --> 00:05:43,520
expect to make to
the plan of record,

150
00:05:43,520 --> 00:05:45,970
like a snapshot of
the before and after.

151
00:05:45,970 --> 00:05:48,500
Then go into in-depth
proposal for

152
00:05:48,500 --> 00:05:51,290
the necessary changes and
address any trade-offs.

153
00:05:51,290 --> 00:05:54,065
Finally, provide any
background information

154
00:05:54,065 --> 00:05:56,815
so that everyone shares
the same context.

155
00:05:56,815 --> 00:06:00,080
You can also refer to your
statement of work, or SOW,

156
00:06:00,080 --> 00:06:01,790
for more information about who

157
00:06:01,790 --> 00:06:04,120
needs to be involved
in that conversation.

158
00:06:04,120 --> 00:06:06,200
If you find that one or more of

159
00:06:06,200 --> 00:06:08,990
your milestones are at risk
of not being completed,

160
00:06:08,990 --> 00:06:10,400
then you'll need
to get a customer

161
00:06:10,400 --> 00:06:12,200
sign-off before the scope,

162
00:06:12,200 --> 00:06:14,030
deadline, or budget are changed,

163
00:06:14,030 --> 00:06:17,140
and all parties involved
need to be informed.

164
00:06:17,140 --> 00:06:19,640
Great work. We've reviewed how

165
00:06:19,640 --> 00:06:21,770
to define risk and
know more about how

166
00:06:21,770 --> 00:06:23,750
to identify the reasons why risks

167
00:06:23,750 --> 00:06:26,270
and changes might occur
during a project.

168
00:06:26,270 --> 00:06:28,055
And now we can explain the impact

169
00:06:28,055 --> 00:06:29,830
of increasing a project scope.

170
00:06:29,830 --> 00:06:31,490
In the next video, we're going

171
00:06:31,490 --> 00:06:33,080
to discuss the role dependencies

172
00:06:33,080 --> 00:06:34,520
play and how to properly

173
00:06:34,520 --> 00:06:37,470
manage them. We'll
meet you there.
